\title{pgmcompR: Probabilistic Graphical Models in Native R}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

A unified framework for probabilistic graphical models written in R. Provides first class discrete and Gaussian factors with an explicit variable registry, Bayesian networks with tabular, linear Gaussian, conditional Gaussian and custom conditional distributions, Markov networks, factor graphs, junction trees and dynamic Bayesian networks. Inference includes enumeration, variable elimination with several elimination orderings, junction tree calibration, sum product and max product belief propagation, loopy belief propagation with damping, maximum a posteriori and most probable explanation queries, rejection sampling, likelihood weighting, importance sampling, Gibbs sampling with convergence diagnostics, forward backward smoothing, Viterbi decoding, particle filtering and Kalman filtering. Parameters are estimated by maximum likelihood, Dirichlet posterior estimation or expectation maximization; structures are learned by the PC algorithm, hill climbing, tabu search and max min hill climbing. Causal tools cover d-separation, backdoor and frontdoor adjustment, interventional queries by truncated factorization and counterfactuals in discrete structural causal models. Models can be read and written as BIF, XMLBIF, UAI and JSON, and converted to and from 'bnlearn', 'gRain' and 'igraph'. Every algorithm has a pure R implementation; optional compiled kernels accelerate factor algebra.
}
